# A Cloudflare API token. https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens
#
# Create a CUSTOM TOKEN rather than using a template, and rather than using the Global API
# Key, which this package does not support at all: the global key cannot be scoped, cannot be
# revoked on its own, and grants everything the account can do to whatever holds it.
#
# The narrowest token that runs the read-only exercises is one with:
#
#   Permissions:  Zone / DNS / Read
#   Zone Resources:  Include / Specific zone / <your domain>
#
# `verify` also reports the accounts when the token has Account / Account Settings / Read,
# and says so plainly when it does not - a token that only manages DNS is not supposed to be
# able to enumerate the account it sits in.
#
# The `records` exercise needs Zone / DNS / Edit, and writes to real DNS. See below.
CLOUDFLARE_TOKEN=

# The zone the zone and record exercises work with, by domain name. Nothing here guesses one:
# an exercise that picked "the first zone on the account" would do something different on
# every account it ran against, and on one of them that would be the wrong domain.
#
# For the `records` exercise this MUST be a zone you own and are willing to have a throwaway
# TXT record appear in for a few seconds.
CLOUDFLARE_DOMAIN=example.com

# There are two more variables the harness reads, and they are deliberately NOT listed above,
# because this file is meant to be copied to .env and one of them must never live there:
#
#   CLOUDFLARE_WRITE_RECORDS=yes              the `records` exercise may write to real DNS
#   CLOUDFLARE_AGENT_MAY_WRITE_RECORDS=1      ...even in an agent session
#
# The first is the ordinary opt-in and may sit in .env if you accept what it does. The second
# unlocks it for an agent, and belongs on the command line for a single run - written into
# .env it stops being a deliberate act, which is the whole of what it is for.
#
# `yes` rather than `1` for the first, so it cannot be set by habit alongside some other
# switch. What it permits: one TXT record created under a name like
# zz-delete-me-cloudflare-api-harness-<timestamp> in CLOUDFLARE_DOMAIN, read back, patched,
# replaced and deleted in a `finally`. A record in a live zone is served to the whole internet
# for as long as it exists, so "safe" here means "does not break the zone", not "invisible".
